#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:0
msgid ""
"Use snapctl within snaps to manage configuration, confdb, components, "
"health, interfaces, services, mounts, models, and refreshes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:8
msgid "Use snapctl"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:10
msgid ""
"The `snapctl` tool is bundled with _snapd_ to provide specific environmental "
"feedback and a limited set of controls from **within the context** of a "
"snap's execution environment in relation to snapd. It can be used, for "
"example, to retrieve and set snap configuration options, check the status of "
"a running snap, and reboot an Ubuntu Core 20 environment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:12
msgid ""
"`snapctl` is typically run from a script _within a snap_, rather than on the "
"host system. These scripts are used by snap developers to implement {ref}"
"`hooks <reference-development-supported-snap-hooks>`, or from within "
"[snapcraft.yaml](https://documentation.ubuntu.com/snapcraft/stable/reference/"
"project-file/snapcraft-yaml/), to augment a snap's execution environment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:14
msgid ""
"For details on using _snapctl_ to add user options to a snap, see {ref}"
"`Adding snap configuration <how-to-guides-manage-snaps-set-system-options>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:16
msgid "Configuration options"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:18
msgid ""
"A snap's configuration options can be queried and altered with the `snapctl "
"get`, `snapctl set` and `snapctl unset` commands. These work very similar to "
"the analogous {ref}`snap get/set/.. commands <how-to-guides-work-with-snaps-"
"configure-snaps>` outside the snap. The main difference is that using these "
"commands from within a snap will *not* trigger {ref}`the configure hook "
"<reference-development-supported-snap-hooks>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:20
msgid ""
"The `snapctl` command uses the same get, set and unset syntax as the snap "
"command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:22
msgid "snapctl get <configuration option>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:26
msgid "For example, the following sets a value of `80` for *http*:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:28
msgid "snapctl set ports.http=80\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:32
msgid ""
"To unset a value, pass its name with `snapctl unset`, and more than one "
"value can be passed at a time:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:34
msgid "snapctl unset ports.http ports.https\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:38
msgid ""
"For convenience, an option can also be unset by adding an exclamation mark "
"(`!`) to the end of a value. For example, the following *unsets* `https`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:40
msgid "snapctl set ports.http=80 ports.https!\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:44
msgid ""
"By using a dot in the key of an option, you create a nested configuration. "
"You can retrieve multiple nested options by specifying their common key:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:46
msgid ""
"$ snapctl get ports\n"
"{\n"
"\"http\": \"80\",\n"
"\"https\": \"443\"\n"
"}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:54
msgid ""
"To see this in action, look at the [NextCloud snap](https://github.com/"
"nextcloud/nextcloud-snap). It uses `snapctl` within its [various hooks]"
"(https://github.com/nextcloud/nextcloud-snap/blob/master/src/hooks/utilities/"
"configuration-utilities) to set configuration options such as `snapctl get "
"private.mode` and `snapctl set private.mode=\"$1\"`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:56
msgid "Confdb"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:58
msgid ""
"The `snapctl get`, `snapctl set` and `snapctl unset` commands can also be "
"used to access and modify {ref}`confdb configurations <explanation-how-snaps-"
"work-confdb-configuration-mechanism>`. To use `snapctl` to access confdb, "
"you can include the `--view` flag as well as the name of the snap's {ref}"
"`interface plug <explanation-interfaces-all-about-interfaces>` that refers "
"to the [confdb view](https://documentation.ubuntu.com/core/reference/"
"assertions/confdb-schema/) being accessed, prefixed with a colon."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:60
msgid ""
"$ snapctl get --view :setup-wifi ssid\n"
"foo\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:65
msgid ""
"When using `snapctl get` in {ref}`confdb hooks <ref-confdb-configuration-"
"mechanism_hooks>`, the data returned will reflect the changes being "
"committed as part of the ongoing transaction that resulted in the hooks "
"being invoked. To read the state of the confdb without the uncommitted "
"changes, you can use the `--previous` flag."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:67
msgid ""
"$ snapctl get --view --previous :setup-wifi ssid\n"
"bar\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:72
msgid ""
"You can also use the `--default` flag to provide a default value to be "
"returned if no data is stored under the requested configuration path."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:74
msgid ""
"If the data being requested can be filtered, due to the path specifying "
"placeholders or field filters, we can constrain the filter parameters using "
"the `--with` flag:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:76
msgid ""
"$ snapctl get --view :setup-wifi routes --with to=default\n"
"{\n"
"    \"enp3s0\": {\n"
"        \"to\": \"default\",\n"
"        \"via\": \"10.12.34.3\",\n"
"        \"metric\": 100\n"
"    },\n"
"    \"eth0\": {\n"
"        \"to\": \"default\",\n"
"        \"via\": \"10.0.0.1\",\n"
"        \"metric\": 200\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:92
msgid ""
"Concurrent accesses may block if they would result in writes running "
"concurrently with another access. If any access is blocked, subsequent "
"accesses will also be queued to ensure that they are run in order."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:94
msgid ""
"For example, if there is an ongoing read and a write is requested, the write "
"will be blocked. A new read would then also be queued and only run after the "
"write finishes, even though reads are normally run concurrently. This "
"ensures that a stream of reads cannot starve a write access."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:96
msgid ""
"The `--wait-for` flag can be used to specify a timeout after which the "
"command will stop waiting for other accesses to complete and cancel the "
"request."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:98
msgid ""
"For further information on confdb, see {ref}`Configure snaps with confdb "
"<how-to-guides-manage-snaps-configure-snaps-with-confdb>` and {ref}`Confdb "
"configuration mechanism <explanation-how-snaps-work-confdb-configuration-"
"mechanism>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:101
msgid "Components"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:103
msgid "Component support requires *snapd 2.67+* ."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:105
msgid ""
"{ref}`Components <explanation-how-snaps-work-snap-components>` are exposed "
"in the snap mountspace under `/snap/<snap_name>/components/<snap_revision>/"
"<component_name>`. This is a symlink to `/snap/<snap_name>/components/mnt/"
"<component_name>/<component_revision>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:107
msgid ""
"There is only one component revision active for a snap revision at a given "
"time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:109
msgid ""
"From within a snap, the _snapctl_ command can be used to install and remove "
"components from an application, including snap hooks and component hooks. "
"The syntax for both commands are as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:111
msgid ""
"snapctl install +<comp_name>\n"
"snapctl remove +<comp_name>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:116
msgid ""
"If these commands are run from a {ref}`hook <reference-development-supported-"
"snap-hooks>`, the components will be installed/removed after the hook itself "
"has run if it ended successfully."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:118
msgid "Asynchronous Operation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:119
msgid ""
"By default, the `install` and `remove` sub-commands run synchronously and "
"will block until the change they create is completed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:121
msgid ""
"The `--no-wait` flag can be used with `install` and `remove` to indicate "
"that snapctl should not block on the created change. Instead, snapctl will "
"immediately return the change ID. This has similar semantics to `--no-wait` "
"when used with `snap install/refresh`. This change ID should be used with "
"the `is-ready` and `tasks` sub-commands. Note that `--no-wait` is not "
"allowed to be used within a hook."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:123
msgid ""
"The `is-ready` sub-command enables polling the given change until it has "
"reached a terminal state. See `snapctl is-ready --help` for a detailed "
"breakdown of the exit codes used."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:125
msgid ""
"The `tasks` sub-command prints detailed information about the given change. "
"This includes information such as the exact state of the change and the "
"progress of individual tasks within the change. Provide the `--format=json` "
"argument to receive JSON output, rather than the default tabular "
"representation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:127
msgid ""
"Note that both `snapctl is-ready` and `snapctl tasks` can only be used with "
"changes that were created by the snap in which they are being invoked. "
"Arbitrary changes on the system cannot be introspected via these sub-"
"commands."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:129
msgid "Health state"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:131
msgid ""
"ⓘ  Health reporting is under development and its capabilities and syntax may "
"change."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:133
msgid ""
"Snap developers can use `snapctl set-health` to provide feedback on the "
"operational state, or health, of a snap."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:135
msgid "It uses the following syntax:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:137
msgid "snapctl set-health [--code=<error code>] <status> [<message>]\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:141
msgid "`status` can be one of the following:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:143
msgid "`okay`: which takes no message and no code"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:144
msgid ""
"`waiting`: some resource the snap needs isn't ready yet, and there's nothing "
"for the user to do but wait. A message (+code) must explain what it's "
"waiting for"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:145
msgid ""
"`blocked`: the user needs to do something for the snap to do something. A "
"message (+code) must say what"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:146
msgid ""
"`error`: something went wrong; a message (+code) must explain what has broken"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:148
msgid ""
"Outside the snap, health status in included as a note in the output to `snap "
"list`, and as a category in `snap info` for a specific snap:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:150
msgid ""
"$ snap info nextcloud\n"
"name:    nextcloud\n"
"summary: Nextcloud Server - A safe home for all your data\n"
"health:\n"
"  status:  blocked\n"
"  message: Backing up database.\n"
"  checked: today at 10:44 GMT\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:160
msgid ""
"For more comprehensive information on using `snapctl set-health`, see "
"[Health checks](https://forum.snapcraft.io/t/health-checks/10605)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:162
msgid "Interface connections"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:164
msgid "(from _snapd 2.43+_)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:166
msgid ""
"The state of a specific snap interface can be probed with the `snapctl is-"
"connected` sub-command by supplying either a slot or plug name as an "
"argument:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:168
msgid "snapctl is-connected <plug|slot>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:172
msgid ""
"The plug or slot is always the name of the plug/slot from the calling snap."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:174
msgid ""
"If the given plug or slot is connected, the command returns the standard "
"exit code for success, which is `0` on POSIX systems. A non-zero exit code "
"is returned in all other cases."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:176
msgid ""
"For example, the following indicates the camera interface **is not** "
"connected:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:178
msgid ""
"$ snapctl is-connected camera; echo $?\n"
"1\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:183
msgid "This behaviour can be easily used within a hook, for example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:185
msgid ""
"if snapctl is-connected camera; then\n"
"  # exit status=0. logic when connected\n"
"  echo \"connected\"\n"
"else\n"
"  # logic when not connected; note if this is run from hooks.\n"
"  # printing to stdout/stderr is not visible to the user\n"
"  # (unless the hook fails entirely with exit status > 0)\n"
"  echo \"not connected\"\n"
"fi\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:197
msgid ""
"Snaps can only query their own plugs and slots because the snap name is "
"implicit and implied by the snapctl execution context."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:199
msgid ""
"See {ref}`Snapcraft interfaces <ref-index_interfaces>` for more details on "
"manipulating interfaces from a snap."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:201
msgid "Model information"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:203
msgid "(from _snapd 2.56+_ onwards)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:205
msgid ""
"A [model assertion](https://ubuntu.com/core/docs/reference/assertions/model) "
"contains the fundamental definition of a snap-based device, such as a device "
"running [Ubuntu Core](https://ubuntu.com/core/)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:208
msgid ""
"The _snap model_ command can be used to return to the active model "
"identification for the device, and `snapctl model` returns the equivalent to "
"`snap model --verbose` from within a snap. However, for this to work, the "
"requesting snap needs to meet _one_ of the following criteria:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:210
msgid "The requesting snap must be either:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:211
msgid ""
"a {ref}`gadget snap <reference-development-yaml-schemas-the-gadget-snap>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:212
msgid "published from the same brand as the device model assertion"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:213
msgid "have the {ref}`snapd-control <interfaces-snapd-control-interface>` plug"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:215
msgid ""
"By default, the output model identification information is presented in a "
"structured yaml-like format:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:217
msgid ""
"brand-id:      canonical\n"
"model:         ubuntu-core-22-amd64\n"
"grade:         signed\n"
"[...]\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:224
msgid "This can be changed to JSON with the `--json` flag:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:226
msgid ""
"{\n"
"  \"architecture\": \"amd64\",\n"
"  \"base\": \"core22\",\n"
"  \"brand-id\": \"canonical\",\n"
"  \"grade\": \"signed\",\n"
"  \"model\": \"ubuntu-core-22-amd64\",\n"
"  \"serial\": \"5f1ee168-ee21-4c38-b03c-5ff9bef64c1e\",\n"
"  \"snaps\": [\n"
"    {\n"
"      \"default-channel\": \"22/stable\",\n"
"      \"id\": \"UqFziVZDHLSyO3TqSWgNBoAdHbLI4dAH\",\n"
"      \"name\": \"pc\",\n"
"      \"type\": \"gadget\"\n"
"    },\n"
"    [...]\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:244
msgid "The raw assertion can also be requested with the `--assertion` flag."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:246
msgid "Mount control"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:248
msgid ""
"When the {ref}`mount-control interface <interfaces-mount-control-interface>` "
"is connected, a snapped application or service can use the _mount_ command "
"to mount transient (non-persistent) and persistent filesystem mount points:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:250
msgid ""
"snapctl mount -o <options> -t <fstype> </path/to/device> </target/mount/"
"point>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:254
msgid "To mount a persistent mount point, add the `--persistent` option:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:256
msgid "$ snapctl mount --persistent -o bind,rw /usr/share /media/mount\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:260
msgid "A corresponding _umount_ command can be used to remove a mount point:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:262
msgid "snapctl umount </path/to/mount/point>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:266
msgid ""
"See {ref}`mount-control interface <interfaces-mount-control-interface>` for "
"further details on permitted filesystems and mount options."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:268
msgid "Mounts in SNAP_DATA and snap refreshes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:270
msgid ""
"`SNAP_DATA` is revision-specific (see {ref}`Environment variables <reference-"
"development-environment-variables>`): its path changes when a snap refreshes "
"to a new revision. Any mounts under `SNAP_DATA` are therefore not "
"automatically carried over to the new revision's data directory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:272
msgid ""
"If a mount inside `SNAP_DATA` must be preserved across a refresh, the snap "
"developer must manage it explicitly in the snap's refresh hooks:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:274
msgid "The `pre-refresh` hook should unmount it:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:276
msgid "snapctl umount \"$SNAP_DATA/my/mount/point\"\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:280
msgid ""
"The `post-refresh` hook, or an application or service whenever it's run for "
"the first time, can re-establish it under the new revision's path:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:282
msgid ""
"snapctl mount -o <options> -t <fstype> </path/to/device> \"$SNAP_DATA/my/"
"mount/point\"\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:286
msgid ""
"Mounts under `SNAP_COMMON` (`/var/snap/<snap-name>/common`) are not affected "
"by revision changes and do not require this handling."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:289
msgid "Reboot control (from the UC20+ install-device hook)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:291
msgid ""
"The `snapctl reboot` command can be used to control reboot behaviour from "
"the gadget `install-device hook` during UC20+ **install mode**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:293
msgid ""
"See the [UC20+ installation process](https://documentation.ubuntu.com/core/"
"explanation/how-installation-works/#the-install-device-hook) documentation "
"for further details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:295
msgid "Refresh control (from the UC20+ gate-auto-refresh hook)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:297
msgid ""
"The gate-auto-refresh hook is executed by snapd for every snap that will be "
"updated with the next automatic refresh. It's also executed for every snap "
"that is dependent on a snap that will be updated."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:299
msgid ""
"This hook is capable of executing the _snapctl refresh_ command with 3 "
"specific arguments, `hold`, `proceed` and `pending`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:301
msgid ""
"This feature is currently considered experimental. See {ref}`Refresh control "
"<how-to-guides-work-with-snaps-manage-updates>` for further details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:303
msgid "snapctl refresh --hold"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:305
msgid ""
"Requests that snapd does not refresh the calling snap, nor the snaps it "
"depends upon, during the current automatic refresh. A snap can hold its own "
"refresh for up to 90 days and other snaps for up to 48 hours. The command "
"prints an error and returns a non-zero exit status if these deadlines are "
"reached and the refresh can no longer be held."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:307
msgid "snapctl refresh --proceed"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:309
msgid ""
"Signals to snapd that a refresh can proceed for both the calling snap and "
"the snaps it depends upon. This does not necessarily mean the update will "
"happen, because they may be held by other snaps, and snapd only proceeds "
"with auto-refresh after consulting gate-auto-refresh hooks of all "
"potentially affected snaps."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:311
msgid "snapctl refresh --pending"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:313
msgid ""
"Checks whether the executing snap has a pending refresh, or will be affected "
"by the refresh of its base snap."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:315
msgid ""
"The output from `snapctl refresh --pending` includes the following details:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:317
msgid "**pending**: none, inhibited or ready"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:318
#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:332
msgid "**channel**: tracking-channel"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:319
msgid ""
"**version**: version (only if there is a pending refresh for the snap itself)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:320
msgid ""
"**revision**: revision (only if there is a pending refresh for the snap "
"itself)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:321
msgid ""
"**base**: true or false (true if the snap is affected by refresh of its base "
"snap)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:322
msgid "**restart**: true or false (true if refresh will cause system restart)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:324
msgid ""
"The pending output value is set to \"none\" if there is no pending refresh "
"for the snap and the value is \"ready\" if there is a pending refresh. A "
"pending value of \"inhibited\" indicates that the next refresh is inhibited "
"because one or more of the snap's applications are running. This currently "
"requires the experimental refresh app awareness feature to be enabled (see "
"below)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:326
msgid "snapctl refresh --tracking"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:328
msgid "Checks which channel the snap is currently tracking."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:330
msgid ""
"The output from `snapctl refresh --tracking` includes the following details:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:334
msgid "The snap-refresh-control interface"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:336
msgid ""
"The `snapctl refresh --proceed` command can be executed by a snapped "
"application outside of the gate-auto-refresh hook if the snap has the `snap-"
"refresh-control` interface and the interface is connected. This enables the "
"snap to trigger an auto-refresh outside of the normal auto-refresh schedule "
"and should be used cautiously."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:338
msgid ""
"Please note that the  \"snapctl refresh\" commands cannot be used from hooks "
"other than gate-auto-refresh hook."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:340
msgid ""
"If the gate-auto-refresh hook doesn't invoke \"snapctl refresh --proceed\" "
"or \"snapctl refresh --hold\" commands and exits with exit code 0, the "
"refresh proceeds normally as if the hook didn't exist (except for respecting "
"\"inhibited\" status if refresh app awareness is in use)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:342
msgid ""
"If the hook fails with an error, snapd assumes \"hold\" as long as the "
"maximum deadline hasn't been reached."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:344
msgid "Services"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:346
msgid ""
"As with configuration options (see above), snapctl sub-commands for managing "
"services are the same as those used by the snap command. See {ref}`Services "
"and daemons <how-to-guides-manage-snaps-control-services>` for further "
"details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:348
msgid ""
"To query the startup and running state of a service, for example, use "
"`snapctl services <service-name>`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:350
msgid ""
"$ snapctl services nextcloud.mysql\n"
"Service          Startup  Current  Notes\n"
"nextcloud.mysql  enabled  active   -\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:356
msgid ""
"The `start`, `stop` and `restart` snapctl commands can be used to start, "
"stop and restart services:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:358
msgid ""
"$ snapctl stop nextcloud.mysql\n"
"$ snapctl services nextcloud.mysql\n"
"Service          Startup  Current   Notes\n"
"nextcloud.mysql  enabled  inactive  -\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:365
msgid ""
"Services can be enabled and disabled by adding the `--enable` argument to "
"_snapctl start_ and `--disable` to _snapctl stop_ respectively:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:367
msgid ""
"$ snapctl start nextcloud.myql --enable\n"
"$ snapctl stop nextcloud.mysql --disable\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:372
msgid "Snaps can only query their own services."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:374
msgid "System mode"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:376
msgid ""
"The `snapctl system-mode` command returns YAML-formatted details about "
"specific system states:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:378
msgid ""
"$ snapctl system-mode\n"
"system-mode: install\n"
"seed-loaded: true\n"
"factory: true\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:385
msgid "The following keys and values can potentially be returned:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:387
msgid ""
"**system-mode**: `install`, `factory-reset`, `recover`, `run`</br>  The "
"current (operational) system mode:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:387
#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:392
#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:396
#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:398
msgid "</br>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:389
msgid "`install`: denotes the system is installing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:390
msgid "`factory-reset`: a factory reset has been triggered"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:391
msgid "`recover`: the system is in _recovery mode_"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:392
msgid ""
"`run`: indicates the system has booted normally.</br>This is the only "
"reported system mode on UC16/UC18 system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:394
msgid ""
"See [Recovery modes](https://ubuntu.com/core/docs/recovery-modes) for more "
"details on each mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:396
msgid ""
"**seed-loaded**: `true` </br>   Set when the installation of seeded snaps "
"for the model has finished."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/snap-development/use-snapctl.md:398
msgid ""
"**factory**: `true`</br>  Only possible on a [UC20+](https://ubuntu.com/core/"
"docs/uc20) system in install mode (`system-mode: install`) with the factory "
"image hint set. This  value can be used to govern whether factory-only "
"resources may be available. See [Factory image hint](https://"
"documentation.ubuntu.com/core/explanation/how-installation-works/) for more "
"details."
msgstr ""
